{
  "schema": "be-mvp-breach-v1",
  "spdx": "GPL-3.0-only",
  "copyright": "Copyright (C) 2026 James Morris <jmorris@namei.org>",
  "purpose": "The hostile-monitor attempts of the board's image for the second VS-domain hart, k3-breach-g1 (planning/mvp-m11-board-addendum.md, B-10): anchor's breach-g1 rows as M11-7 declares them (scenarios/anchor/breach.json, with VS domain 1's own rows in place), but for the rows on devices, which use the board's register forms and name only the devices that B-10 lists, with no witness; the start and status rows of harts 8 and 15 (N1 to N3) in the places of the rows on the S-level interrupt controller and the power device, which the board's image does not name; and M-mode's memory at the firmware's place on the board. effects says, for each row on a device or M-mode's memory and each new call, what it would do on the board if the boundary failed: nothing that outlasts a reset. Each row's at is the physical target; tools/gen-breach.py makes the access's address from it by the row's mode.",
  "trust": "Test data. Beside the candidate's checks of each row against the hooked image's descriptor, tools/gen-breach.py's board rules (M11-B) require each of these devices to be mvp/k3/platform.json's, and refuse a row whose bytes, or a call argument named as an address, meet any device of the board's tree but B-10's five (stop B9), or lie outside them, the board's memory beyond the first 32 MiB, and the firmware's place, with the reason of the most specific kind.",
  "scope": "The board's k3-breach-g1 image alone, under QEMU's k3-like machine and in M11-B's second sitting; it has no -off copy.",
  "platform": "k3-com260",
  "role": "vs-domain",
  "domain": 1,
  "devices": {
    "console": "0xd4017000",
    "console-scratch": "0x1c",
    "console-scratch-width": 4,
    "aplic-m": "0xf1800000",
    "imsic-m": "0xf1000000",
    "imsic-s": "0xe0400000",
    "imsic-s-stride": "0x40000",
    "mtime": "0xf181bff8",
    "mtimecmp": "0xf1814000",
    "timer-width": 4,
    "m-memory": "0x100000000",
    "unnamed-ram": "0x200000000"
  },
  "effects": {
    "I1": "a 4-byte store of 0xa5 to the console's scratch register, which nothing reads: it holds the value until a reset",
    "I5": "a store of the hook's pattern word to hart 0's S-level interrupt file, its seteipnum register: the word is no identity that the file implements (511), which the AIA has the file ignore; at most an interrupt pending on hart 0 until it is taken or a reset",
    "I6": "as I5, to the S-level interrupt file of the other VS domain's hart",
    "I8": "an instruction fetch at the console's base, its receive register: at most one received byte taken from the console's FIFO",
    "J2": "a store of the pattern word into the firmware's text in memory: the firmware is wrong until a reset, after which the boot loads it again from the card",
    "J3": "the firmware's text run in HS-mode: at most stores to the firmware's own memory before its first M-mode register traps as an illegal instruction; the boot loads the firmware again",
    "J5": "a 4-byte store of the pattern word to the low half of hart 0's mtimecmp: hart 0's next M-mode timer interrupt comes at another time, until the firmware sets the compare again",
    "J7": "a store of the pattern word to the M-level APLIC's domaincfg: at most the domain's interrupt enable cleared until the next boot sets it; the domain delegates all its sources to the S-level domain",
    "J8": "a store of the pattern word to hart 0's M-level interrupt file: no identity that the file implements (511), which the AIA has the file ignore",
    "J12": "a store into memory that no extent names and that no boot reads",
    "N1": "had the call list failed, hart 8 would start at the park entry, the monitor's code that holds a hart, until a reset",
    "N3": "had the call list failed, hart 15 would start at the park entry, until a reset"
  },
